# throttlr

A Redis-backed rate limiting middleware for Express, with pluggable algorithms — fixed window, sliding window, and token bucket — chosen via config, not hardcoded.

Built as a standalone, npm-installable package rather than app-specific code, so it can drop into any Express project.

---

## Why

Most rate limiter tutorials hardcode one algorithm and call it done. `throttlr` implements all three of the common approaches so you can pick the right tradeoff for your use case, and exposes a consistent middleware API regardless of which one you choose.

| Algorithm | Good for | Tradeoff |
|---|---|---|
| **Fixed window** | Simple APIs, low overhead | Allows bursts at window boundaries |
| **Sliding window** | Smooth, accurate limiting | Slightly more Redis memory (stores timestamps) |
| **Token bucket** | Allowing short bursts while enforcing an average rate | Slightly more complex refill logic |

---

## Installation

```bash
npm install throttlr
```

Requires a running Redis instance (v5+). See [Local Redis setup](#local-redis-setup) below if you don't have one.

---

## Quick start

```js
const express = require("express");
const Redis = require("ioredis");
const { rateLimiter } = require("throttlr");

const app = express();
const redis = new Redis(); // defaults to localhost:6379

app.use(
  rateLimiter({
    redis,
    algorithm: "sliding-window", // "fixed-window" | "sliding-window" | "token-bucket"
    windowMs: 60_000,            // 1 minute
    max: 100,                    // 100 requests per window per key
  })
);

app.get("/", (req, res) => res.send("ok"));

app.listen(3000);
```

By default, requests are throttled by `req.ip`. You can key on anything (user ID, API key, etc.) via `keyGenerator`.

---

## Configuration

| Option | Type | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `redis` | `Redis` | — | An `ioredis` client instance (required) |
| `algorithm` | `string` | `"fixed-window"` | `"fixed-window"`, `"sliding-window"`, or `"token-bucket"` |
| `windowMs` | `number` | `60000` | Window size in milliseconds (fixed/sliding window) |
| `max` | `number` | `100` | Max requests allowed per window |
| `refillRate` | `number` | — | Tokens added per second (token bucket only) |
| `bucketSize` | `number` | — | Max tokens the bucket can hold (token bucket only) |
| `keyGenerator` | `(req) => string` | `req => req.ip` | How to identify a client |
| `onLimitReached` | `(req, res) => void` | sends `429` | Custom handler when a request is blocked |

Every response includes standard rate-limit headers:

```
X-RateLimit-Limit: 100
X-RateLimit-Remaining: 87
X-RateLimit-Reset: 1735000060
```

---

## How each algorithm works

**Fixed window** — counts requests in a discrete time bucket (e.g. `12:00:00–12:01:00`) using `INCR` + `EXPIRE`. Cheapest option; can allow up to 2x the limit right at a window boundary.

**Sliding window** — stores a timestamp per request in a Redis sorted set, trims anything older than the window, and counts what's left. Accurate, no boundary bursting, costs a bit more memory.

**Token bucket** — each key has a bucket that refills at a steady rate and drains one token per request, computed atomically in a Lua script. Allows short bursts up to the bucket size while enforcing a long-run average rate.

---

## Local Redis setup

**WSL / Linux:**
```bash
sudo apt install redis-server
sudo service redis-server start
redis-cli ping   # should return PONG
```

**Mac:**
```bash
brew install redis
brew services start redis
```

**No local install:** use a free hosted instance from [Upstash](https://upstash.com) and point `ioredis` at the connection URL it gives you.

---

## Development

```bash
git clone https://github.com/anujtaywade/throttlr.git
cd throttlr
npm install
npm test
```

Project structure:

```
throttlr/
├── packages/
│   └── middleware/        # the actual npm package
│       ├── src/
│       │   ├── algorithms/
│       │   │   ├── fixedWindow.js
│       │   │   ├── slidingWindow.js
│       │   │   └── tokenBucket.js
│       │   ├── redisClient.js
│       │   └── index.js
│       └── test/
└── demo-app/               # small Express app exercising the middleware
```

